WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.759
The final point I want to make about this is a sobering one,

00:03.759 --> 00:07.519
which is about not getting caught up in the hype

00:07.519 --> 00:09.839
of agentic coding.

00:09.839 --> 00:14.199
Now look, generally I am a huge fan, very passionate

00:14.199 --> 00:17.280
about the impact that LLMs can make to software development,

00:17.280 --> 00:20.039
but there is certainly some hype out there

00:20.039 --> 00:21.800
that is unwarranted.

00:21.800 --> 00:25.359
There are some tasks for sure where using LLMs

00:25.359 --> 00:28.559
can make it an order of magnitude faster

00:28.559 --> 00:29.559
to deliver.

00:29.559 --> 00:33.880
If you're building a boilerplate React frontend, which is going to evolve a lot of

00:33.880 --> 00:38.159
componentry and you've got a very good sense of what you want the UI to look like, an

00:38.159 --> 00:43.799
LLM can do that in a matter of minutes when it would take a great frontend person, maybe

00:43.799 --> 00:46.119
a day and it would take me several weeks.

00:46.119 --> 00:49.600
So certainly for me, order of magnitude difference.

00:49.600 --> 00:56.840
But there are some tasks where LLMs are only incrementally faster than just doing it yourself.

00:56.840 --> 01:01.899
And indeed, I've had experiences where I feel like it has actually slowed me down

01:01.899 --> 01:14.239
because I had to go in and rewrite something where there was a mistake, a very subtle mistake that I wasn't expecting. Netnet, there is no question that LLMs are a huge value add and

01:14.239 --> 01:20.159
that I'm able to achieve a lot more as a result of using them. How much more depends critically

01:20.159 --> 01:26.239
on the project. More highly innovative projects with larger code bases and the improvement becomes

01:26.239 --> 01:33.200
more incremental. Starting out with something completely new where it's a complete Greenfields project, and where I'm

01:33.200 --> 01:37.120
working on something where there's plenty of boilerplate code in there, like, as I say,

01:37.120 --> 01:43.760
a sort of boilerplate React frontend. That's the kind of thing where LLMs can change the game.

01:44.879 --> 01:57.760
Days turn into minutes. It can be that radical. So there are both extremes there as in aggregate for me, it is a multiplier but it's not a 10X and that's

01:57.760 --> 02:04.239
something which I think we all have to have that kind of very reasonable grounded opinion and be

02:04.239 --> 02:10.240
sure to set the record straight if people, there's people that are that are too caught up in the hype

02:10.240 --> 02:18.319
and there are also now there's a sort of movement of people who are anti-AI, perhaps because they've got frustrated by the hype

02:18.319 --> 02:20.520
and they've had some negative experiences.

02:20.520 --> 02:25.719
So it's encumbered on us to do the work, to learn about these tools, to use them and

02:25.719 --> 02:27.599
then to set the record straight.

02:27.599 --> 02:32.000
Both the pros and the cons be able to explain where they are highly beneficial and where

02:32.000 --> 02:33.879
they still have some room to grow.

